<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Owen Roe O'Neill</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1590–<a href="/years/1649">1649</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Eoghan Ruadh Ó Néill</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Owen Roe O'Neill was an Irish soldier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-neill-dynasty">O'Neill dynasty</a> of Ulster who served for decades in the Spanish army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. He returned in 1642 to command the Ulster army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederate-ireland">Confederate Ireland</a> and defeated a Scottish Covenanter army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-benburb">Battle of Benburb</a> in 1646. He died in 1649, shortly after Cromwell's invasion of Ireland began.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/owen-roe-oneill.jpg" alt="Owen Roe O'Neill" width="320" height="363" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, Van Brugens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Owen_Roe_O%27Neill.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Owen Roe was the illegitimate son of Art MacBaron O'Neill, a younger half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-oneill-earl-of-tyrone">Hugh O'Neill, Earl of Tyrone</a>. He was probably born near Loughgall in County Armagh, where his father held estates, and his mother was a daughter of Hugh Connolly O'Reilly, lord of Breifne O'Reilly. Educated by Franciscan monks, he fought as a teenager against the English in the Nine Years' War, and he probably left Ireland in 1607 during the Flight of the Earls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He served for 40 years in the Irish regiment of the Spanish army, mostly against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and against the French. At the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> in 1640 he held out for 48 days with 2,000 men against a French army of 35,000, and he became known as an expert in defensive warfare. In 1627 he proposed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> invade Ireland and make it a republic under Spanish protection; the plan came to nothing.</p><p>After the Irish Rebellion of 1641 he sailed home on the frigate St. Francis, bought with money from the Pope, and on 8 July 1642 he was recognised at Doe Castle as head of the Ulster Irish. His aim was a fully independent Catholic Ireland and the overturning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster">Plantation of Ulster</a>, which set him apart from the Old English leaders of the Catholic Confederation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a> and from his rival Thomas Preston. On 5 June 1646 he routed Major-General Robert Monro at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-benburb">Battle of Benburb</a>, killing or capturing 3,000 Scots.</p><p>Summoned south by the Papal Nuncio, Giovanni Battista Rinuccini, he could not follow up the victory, and he and Preston failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. He rejected the peace the Confederates made with the Royalists so firmly that in 1648 his army fought other Irish Catholic armies, and he relieved the Parliamentarian Charles Coote at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Derry</a> in return for 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> landed in August 1649 he turned again to the 1st Marquess of Ormond.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 6 November 1649 at Cloughoughter Castle in County Cavan. Some believed he was poisoned, but it is now generally accepted that he died of complications of gout. His death was kept secret for a time. The Ulster army passed to Heber MacMahon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Clogher, and was destroyed at the Battle of Scarrifholis in 1650. In the 19th century the Young Irelanders honoured him as a patriot, and Thomas Davis wrote The Lament for Owen Roe.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Owen Roe O'Neill” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Owen Roe O'Neill</b> won the Battle of Benburb in 1646, while Thomas Preston led the Leinster army.</blockquote><a href="/state/confederate-ireland">Confederate Ireland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in County Armagh, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Cavan, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.35,-6.65&amp;year=1628" aria-label="County Armagh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4403 301 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Owen Roe O'Neill"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4853 571h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4853 571h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.35,-6.65&amp;year=1628">What was here in 1628? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
